From: Zynq bring-up project
Subject: [PATCH] net: macb: leave Zynq GMII transmit clock rate to PL

On Zynq-7000 the GMII transmit clock enters the PS through EMIO.
The GMII-to-RGMII bridge generates and selects it according to the PHY
speed. The PS clock handle is still required to keep the GEM gate enabled,
but its internal divider does not control the external EMIO clock rate.

With an unmodelled PL clock input the Zynq clock driver reports zero Hz.
Trying to round/set that rate produces misleading warnings at link-up.
Do not remove tx_clk from DT: unused-clock shutdown then disables the
GEM gate and traffic stops despite the PHY reporting link-up.

Keep the normal enable/disable lifecycle; skip only rate programming for
Zynq GMII. Other platforms and interfaces retain their existing behavior.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [board bring-up downstream workaround]
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
@@ -528,6 +528,14 @@ static void macb_set_tx_clk(struct macb *bp, int speed)
 	if (bp->phy_interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_MII)
 		return;
 
+	/* Zynq GMII takes its TX clock from PL over EMIO. Keep tx_clk
+	 * enabled for the GEM gate, but leave its rate to the PL bridge.
+	 */
+	if (bp->phy_interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_GMII &&
+	    of_device_is_compatible(bp->pdev->dev.of_node,
+				    "xlnx,zynq-gem"))
+		return;
+
 	rate = rgmii_clock(speed);
 	if (rate < 0)
 		return;
